## Why This Is Asked
The Trie with prefix count mechanism addresses dynamic dictionary requirements in real-time applications like chat search, testing understanding of per-node frequency tracking, deletion correctness, and memory efficiency trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with passCount and endCount for frequency tracking
- O(length) time complexity for all operations
- Memory management through node pruning
- Correct handling of duplicates and edge cases
- Amortized deletion: pruning happens during erase, no separate cleanup needed

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.pass = 0;
    this.end = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass++;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
        node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.pass++;
    }
    node.end++;
  }

  exists(word) {
    const node = this._findNode(word);
    return node !== null && node.end > 0;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    const node = this._findNode(prefix);
    return node ? node.pass : 0;
  }

  erase(word) {
    if (!this.exists(word)) return false;

    let node = this.root;
    node.pass--;

    for (const ch of word) {
      const child = node.children.get(ch);
      child.pass--;
      if (child.pass === 0) {
        node.children.delete(ch);
        node = null;
        break;
      }
      node = child;
    }

    if (node) node.end--;
    return true;
  }

  _findNode(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return null;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node;
  }
}

// Usage
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('app');
trie.exists('apple');        // true
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 3
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // true (still one left)
trie.countPrefix('app');     // 2
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // false
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 1
trie.erase('nonexistent');   // false (safe)
```

## Memory Management
Nodes are allocated only when needed and automatically pruned when `pass` reaches zero during erase, ensuring no memory leaks.

## Duplicate Handling
Since `end` tracks word frequency, duplicate inserts increment the counter; duplicate erases decrement it until the count reaches zero.

## Edge Cases
- Erase on non-existent word: returns false, no changes
- Erase on word with duplicate: only decrements count, preserves other instances
- Empty string handling: root node's counters track this case

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP, memoization, and bottom-up tabulation on a classic polygon triangulation problem with solution counting. It checks careful state design and combinatorial counting under optimal substructure.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on polygons: dp[i][j] = min over i<k<j of dp[i][k] + dp[k][j] + w[i]*w[k]*w[j]
- Counting optimal solutions: parallel cnt[i][j] updated when a new min is found or matched
- Circular handling: fix a root vertex and solve a linearized version

## Code Example
```javascript
function minTriangulation(w){
  const n = w.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const cnt = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n-1;i++){ dp[i][i+1] = 0; cnt[i][i+1] = 1; }
  for(let len=2; len<n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len<n; i++){
      const j = i+len;
      for(let k=i+1; k<j; k++){
        const val = dp[i][k] + dp[k][j] + w[i]*w[k]*w[j];
        if(val < dp[i][j]){ dp[i][j] = val; cnt[i][j] = cnt[i][k] * cnt[k][j]; }
        else if(val === dp[i][j]){ cnt[i][j] += cnt[i][k] * cnt[k][j]; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {minCost: dp[0][n-1], ways: cnt[0][n-1]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design DP states that encode ordering constraints across variable-length tokens, a common real-world pattern in data parsing and tokenization.

## Key Concepts
- DP state design with (pos, prevLen) to compare adjacent tokens
- String comparison for equal-length numbers; no leading zeros handling
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs for clarity and performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWays(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const memo = new Map();
  const dfs = (pos, prevLen) => {
    if (pos === n) return 1;
    const key = pos + ':' + prevLen;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    let res = 0;
    for (let nextLen = 1; nextLen <= n - pos; nextLen++) {
      if (s[pos] === '0') break;
      if (prevLen === 0 || nextLen > prevLen || (nextLen === prevLen && s.substring(pos, pos + nextLen) > s.substring(pos - prevLen, pos))) {
        res = (res + dfs(pos + nextLen, nextLen)) % mod;
      }
    }
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  };
  return dfs(0, 0);
}
```

